.TH LOGIN 6
.SH NAME
login \- key exchange protocol
.SH DESCRIPTION
The following encrypted key exchange protocol is used between a client such as
.B login
in
.IR security-login (2),
and a certificate signing process such as
.IR logind (8),
to justify the latter's issuing a certificate that can
later be presented to an Inferno service
to establish credentials.
.PP
A shared secret must previously be agreed between
user and certifying authority (CA).
It is used by the protocol to establish a secure channel between user and CA.
.PP
This page describes version 2 of the exchange.
It is Bellovin and Merritt's encrypted key exchange
with the CA's Diffie-Hellman value alone encrypted,
and it is meant to have that protocol's properties:
somebody who watches a login, or takes either end's part in one
without the password, learns nothing that a guessed password can
be checked against, except that each login he takes part in lets him
try one guess; and each end knows the other had the password before
it sends anything more.
The reason for each step is given with it, because the exchange this
replaces lost those properties one step at a time.
.SS Names
.TF secret
.PD
.TP
.I name
is the user's account name.
It is one name: not empty, at most 255 bytes of UTF-8, with no
.B /
or
.BR \e ,
no control character, no blank at either end, and not
.B .
or
.BR .. .
The CA refuses any other before it acknowledges, counts, looks up or
signs anything, and so do
.IR keysrv (4),
.IR signer (8)
and
.IR changelogin (8).
A name is a directory of
.IR keyfs (4),
the key of the CA's count of failed attempts, the owner of a certificate,
and for
.IR signer (8)
a file of the host's; a name such as
.B carol/.
was the same account as
.B carol
to the first and another name to the second.
.TP
.I pw
is the 32-byte SHA-256 of the password, as
.IR changelogin (8)
keeps it.
.TP
.I suite
is 1 or 2.
Suite 1 is ChaCha20 as the keystream and SHA-256 as the hash H, with a group of
2048 bits or more.
Suite 2 is AES-256 in counter mode (as GCM runs it) and SHA-384, with a group of
3072 bits or more.
A client in CNSA mode (\f5/env/cnsamode\fP set) offers and takes only suite 2,
and a CA in CNSA mode refuses suite 1, saying so.
A CA not in CNSA mode takes either.
.TP
.I group
is 14, 15 or 16: RFC 3526's groups of 2048, 3072 and 4096 bits.
Each is a prime
.I p
with
.IR q =( p \-1)/2
also prime, and the generator 2, which is of order
.IR q .
These are the only groups.
Both ends have them, and neither takes a modulus or a generator from the
other: a client that took them from whoever answered could be sent a modulus of 2,
which made the secret one of two values and the sender the signer.
The CA chooses the group: 14 for suite 1 and 15 for suite 2, or a larger one if
.IR logind (8)
was told to.
The client refuses a number not on the list, and a group smaller than its suite allows.
The top 64 bits of each prime are ones, which matters below.
.TP
.I Nc, Ns
are 32 random bytes chosen by the client and by the CA for this conversation.
.TP
.I r0, r1
are the CA's and the client's random exponents, uniform in
.RI 1.. q \-1
(64 random bits more than
.I q
has, reduced).
.TP
.IB len ( p )
is the length of
.I p
in bytes; a number sent is sent big-endian in exactly that many.
.TP
.I secret
is
.BI 2**(2* r0 * r1 ") mod " p \fR,
in
.IB len ( p )
bytes.
.TP
.BI L( m )
is the length of
.I m
as two bytes, big-endian, followed by
.IR m .
.SS Messages
``user→CA xxx'' means that the user
sends the message ``xxx'' to the certifying authority.
Either party can send an error instead of a message at any
point to terminate the protocol.
.IP
.EX
user→CA	name
CA→user	name
.sp 1v
user→CA	hello = 2, suite, Nc
CA→user	reply = 2, suite, group, Ns, mask(±2**r0 mod p)
.sp 1v
user→CA	2**r1 mod p, proofc
CA→user	proofs
.sp 1v
CA→user	CA's public key, alpha, p
user→CA	user's public key
CA→user	user's public key certificate
.EE
.PP
The numbers 2,
.I suite
and
.I group
are one byte each.
The last three lines are sent under
.IR ssl (3)'s
.BR aes_256_gcm ,
each way under its own secret; everything before is in clear.
.SS "The masked value"
The CA computes
.BI 2** r0 " mod " p
and, as a coin falls, sends it or
.I p
less it, under a keystream:
.IP
.EX
PRK = HMAC-H(Nc || Ns, pw)
key || nonce = HKDF-Expand(PRK,
    "infernode login 2 mask" || 0 || suite || group || name, 44)
mask(x) = x XOR stream(key, nonce)
.EE
.PP
HKDF is RFC 5869's;
.I key
is the first 32 bytes and
.I nonce
the last 12.
The stream is the one the suite's AEAD (ChaCha20-Poly1305, AES-256-GCM)
encrypts with, so
.B mask
is that AEAD's ciphertext with its tag left off, and applied twice gives
.I x
back.
.PP
There is no tag, and that is the point of this message.
The CA sends it to anybody who gives a name.
When it was sealed by the AEAD, tag and all, one reply was all that was needed
to try passwords at leisure: the right one was the one whose tag verified.
Now every password turns the reply into
.I some
number of
.IB len ( p )
bytes, and the right one cannot be told from the others:
.IP \(bu
The value sent is uniform over
.RI 2.. p \-2,
and a wrong password gives a number uniform over all strings of that length,
which is outside that range once in 2^64 because of the prime's leading ones.
(A prime not chosen so would rule out a fraction of the guesses with each reply.)
.IP \(bu
.BI 2** r0
alone is always a square mod
.IR p ,
and whether a number is one can be computed.
Without the sign, a wrong password would unmask the reply to a non-square half the time,
and each login watched would halve the list of candidates.
With it the value is a square or not with equal chance, as a wrong guess's is.
The 2 in the secret's exponent removes the sign again.
.IP \(bu
The value is not text: base 64, which the old exchange sent, has only some byte values.
.PP
.I Ns
is in the keystream's derivation so that a keystream is used once whatever the client sends:
with the nonce the client's alone, the same hello sent again had the CA encrypt a new value
under the same keystream.
.I Name,
.I suite
and
.I group
are there so that a reply is of one conversation's terms.
.PP
The client unmasks the value and refuses it unless it is in
.RI 2.. p \-2;
with 0, 1 or
.IR p \-1
the secret would be a number anybody knows.
It reports
.B "name or secret incorrect"
and sends nothing more.
.SS "The proofs"
The client sends
.BI 2** r1 " mod " p
in clear, with a proof that it has the secret.
The CA refuses a value outside
.RI 2.. p \-2
(the range is SP 800-56A's, 5.6.2.3.1):
with 1 the secret was 1 whatever
.I r0
was, with 0 it was 0, with
.IR p \-1
one of two values, and a client that sent one of them was given a certificate
for any name it chose, with no password.
No test of the value's order is needed: in these groups an element in that range
is of order
.I q
or
.RI 2 q ,
and the exponent
.BI 2* r0
leaves one of order
.IR q .
.PP
Both ends then compute
.IP
.EX
th = H("infernode login 2 transcript" ||
       L(name) || L(hello) || L(reply) || L(2**r1 mod p))
PRK = HMAC-H(th, secret || pw)
proofc = HKDF-Expand(PRK, "infernode login 2 client proof", len H)
proofs = HKDF-Expand(PRK, "infernode login 2 signer proof", len H)
key    = HKDF-Expand(PRK, "infernode login 2 session", len H)
.EE
.PP
.I Th
covers everything said, so the two ends agree on the name, the suite, the group,
both nonces and both values, or their proofs differ: a party between them that
changes any of it gets nothing.
.I Pw
is with the secret so that a secret somebody has contrived to know is still not enough.
.PP
The CA checks
.I proofc
before it sends anything made from the secret.
In the old exchange the CA spoke first under the secret's keys, to a client that had proved nothing;
a client that had chosen
.I r1
could then try each password by unmasking the CA's value with it,
raising the result to
.IR r1 ,
and seeing whether the CA's next message opened.
If the proof is wrong the CA answers
.BR "name or secret incorrect" ,
in clear, and the attempt stays counted against the name
(see
.IR logind (8));
a wrong password, a name with no account and an expired entry are all answered so.
If it is right the CA sends
.IR proofs ,
which the client checks: only an end with the password could have made it.
.SS "The rest"
Each end then pushes
.B aes_256_gcm
onto the connection, each way under its own secret, made from
.I key
by
.B secrets
in
.IR security-ssl (2).
The CA sends its public key and the
.I alpha
and
.I p
of its key file
.RI ( createsignerkey (8)).
Those two are not used by this exchange, whatever the file has: they are the
parameters
.IR security-auth (2)
uses between the holders of the CA's certificates, and they now reach the client
from the CA the password has just authenticated, not in clear before it.
The client refuses a
.I p
of fewer than 2048 or more than 8192 bits, an even one, and an
.I alpha
outside
.RI 2.. p \-2.
It makes a key of the CA's kind, sends the public part, and checks that
what comes back is a certificate for that key that verifies with the CA's.
.SS Versions
There is no negotiation, and neither end goes back to the exchange before this one,
whose third message was an initialization vector of 32 or 44 bytes.
A CA sent one answers
.BR "signer speaks login version 2" ;
a client whose hello is refused as that exchange's CA refused anything that was not
its vector reports that the signer speaks the old exchange.
Both ends of a realm must be of the same version.
.SS "What is not claimed"
.I Pw
is all a client needs, so the CA's copy of it in
.IR keyfs (4)
is as good as the password, and it is an unsalted hash: whoever reads the CA's
key file can log in as anybody and can guess passwords from it.
The exchange protects the password on the network, not on the CA.
.PP
Someone who takes the client's part can try one password in each login,
which is what the CA's count of failed attempts is for;
someone who takes the CA's part can try one in each login a user makes to him.
.PP
The certificate lasts as long as the account's entry does, and there is no revocation.
